A||||9||firstfiero||Need computer help..||||5||
Z||000000||firstfiero||12-21-2001||10:51 AM||||    Can anyone tell me how to allocate more memory to my video card? I'm running windows 98. Someone told me I have to go into dos. It's been so long since I touched dos I can't begin to remember any commands.<P>------------------<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by firstfiero (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||12-21-2001||10:53 AM||||Only way is to get more memory (if the card allows) or get a card with more memory.<P>I'm an old DOS guy and the only memory manager I know of is himem.sys and it only opens up a small amount of high memory area for programs. There are some utilities that will create a "swap" that will increase the "virtual" memory of the computer but not for video.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||12-21-2001||11:02 AM||||They may have been referring to some of the motherboards that have built-in video.  With those you can often go into the BIOS and change the amount of main memory that gets shared with the video circuitry.  If you have a separate video card though, you will have to physically add additional memory to it, if it supports that.||||reg||1
Z||000003||firstfiero||12-21-2001||11:03 AM||||     A friend told me that my video card might be only set to use half of what it is capable. Where the heck do I look to find out how much it's using.||||reg||1
Z||000004||firstfiero||12-21-2001||11:05 AM||||      Where do I find bios.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieronv||12-21-2001||11:24 AM||||when you start up your computer it will say hit the del, or f1 or someother key to enter setup then you'll be in the bios setup pages.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||12-21-2001||11:25 AM||||Your BIOS is easy to find.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> Its that fricken "Waldo" guy thats a beast to find.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">  Anybody found him?<P>Anyways, on boot up, either hit DEL, ESC or what ever else it tells you to hit to enter your SETUP.<P>But, you may want to see if you have the most recent DRIVERS for your video card and use any Utilites that your video card comes/came with to tweak the settings of that card.  <P>You can have a 16MB video card and only be using 4MB maximum of its memory because it wasn't set up right with the drivers and/or the utility.  That is, if the video card utility allows you to adjust memory settings.<P>One of our engineering video cards had 32MB, but was only using 1MB because the setting was set at 1MB.  Refreshes to the CADD drawings sucked.  Tweaked it by moving a slide switch in that video cards utility to use 24MB with the remaining 8MB as a cache.  Works real smooth now.  Refreshes are instant.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> <BR><B><I>"The PRE10DR"</I></B>||||reg||12
Z||000007||Pyric||12-21-2001||01:08 PM||||If the video isn't incorporated into the motherboard than there isn't a whole lot you can do.  Win98 tends to monopolize memory usage for your videocard and wont let you change it.  (unless you get a video card with more memory built onto it.)<P>The DDR ones are nice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||12-21-2001||03:04 PM||||my ASUS motherboard allows me to allocate system memory to my video card, since my card only has 16mb.. I allocated 64mb to video.. but I'm not sure it worked, my video sucks.. might be the cr@ppy ATI drivers though. I think my next video card will be a different brand......... one with better support.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000009||maryjane||12-21-2001||08:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>      Where do I find bios.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Follow the above advice to get into setup.<BR>BUT, be very careful about making changes, if you are unfamilar with the items. A screwed up bios can cause a lot of pain & aggravation. When I have a machine running like I want it to, I go in and write down EVERY entry, just as it is shown, on all pages. That way, I can go back and restore the settings if they somehow get awry. Usually only happens if I do it. Bios is usually pretty reliable, tho there are bios viri oput there.  <BR>||||reg||1
